      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 1: DRIFT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="drift">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">How the Fire Goes Out</h2>

        <p>The New Testament's word for the most common form of spiritual loss is not
        rebellion. It is drift.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Therefore we must pay much closer attention to what we have heard, lest we
          drift away from it.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 2:1">Hebrews 2:1</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">pararreō</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">παραρρέω (Hebrews 2:1)</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">To flow past, slip by, drift. The image is
          nautical: a boat that is not held slides past the harbour on the current. It
          requires no decision, no storm, and no act of will — only the absence of
          attention. Note the direction of the metaphor: the ship does not deliberately
          leave. It simply stops being moored, and the water does the rest.</span>
        </div>

        <p>Jesus describes the same phenomenon in his own terms:
        <em>"because lawlessness will be increased, the love of many will grow
        cold"</em> (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 24:12">Matthew 24:12</a>). The
        verb is <em>psygēsetai</em> — to cool, to lose heat. Not to be extinguished by
        an enemy. To cool.</p>

        <p>And the parable of the sower names the specific mechanism most often at
        work: <em>"the cares of the world and the deceitfulness of riches and the
        desires for other things enter in and choke the word"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 4:19">Mark 4:19</a>). Not persecution. Not
        heresy. <strong>Cares, money, and other things you wanted.</strong></p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Diagnostic Fact</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Almost nobody who cools down decides to.</p>
          <p>Which means that waiting until you feel like you have a problem is not a
          strategy, and that the practices which keep a person warm have to be
          scheduled rather than felt into. This is unglamorous and it is the single
          most practically important sentence in this part. The Christian life is
          maintained the way a fire is maintained — by someone putting wood on it in
          the morning, whether or not they feel like it.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 2: EPHESUS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="ephesus">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Ephesus: Everything Right and the Love Gone</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          I know your works, your toil and your patient endurance, and how you cannot
          bear with those who are evil, but have tested those who call themselves
          apostles and are not, and found them to be false. I know you are enduring
          patiently and bearing up for my name's sake, and you have not grown weary.
          But I have this against you, that you have abandoned the love you had at
          first.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 2:2-4">Revelation 2:2–4</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Read the commendation before the rebuke, because the commendation is the
        point. This church worked hard. It endured. It refused to tolerate evil. It
        examined teachers and correctly identified frauds. It had not grown weary. By
        every metric a congregation normally measures, Ephesus was excellent.</p>

        <p>And Christ says something is wrong that would not have shown up on any
        report.</p>

        <p>The verb is <em>aphēkas</em> — you have <em>left</em>, released, let go. The
        same verb is used of nets left behind when the disciples followed Jesus. It is
        not "your love diminished." It is "you put it down."</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Which Love?</span>
          Commentators divide on whether "the love you had at first" is love for Christ
          or love for one another, and the honest answer is that the letter does not
          say and probably does not distinguish. One detail is worth knowing: roughly a
          generation earlier, Paul had written to this same church that he had heard of
          "your love toward all the saints"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:15">Ephesians 1:15</a>) and had prayed
          that they would be rooted and grounded <em>in love</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 3:17">Ephesians 3:17</a>). The thing
          Ephesus was once known for is the thing Christ says it has put down. Whatever
          else the first love is, it is the thing that made them recognisable, and it
          was not their doctrine.
        </div>

        <p>The remedy is three imperatives, and their order is not accidental.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">First</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Remember from where you have fallen</div>
          <p>Memory comes before repentance. Before you can want it back you have to
          know what you had, and the drifted person characteristically cannot
          remember — that is what drift does. This is an instruction to go back
          deliberately: to the early days, the first answered prayer, the first time
          the text opened, the first person you told. Not nostalgia. Evidence
          retrieval. <em>You were warm once, and here is the record of it.</em></p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Second</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Repent</div>
          <p>Christ calls the loss of first love a sin requiring repentance, not a
          season requiring patience. That is a harder word than most treatments of this
          text allow, and it should not be softened: coldness is not merely
          unfortunate. It is something to turn from.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Third</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Do the works you did at first</div>
          <p>Not <em>feel</em> the feelings you felt at first. <strong>Do the
          works.</strong> The prescription is behavioural, and it runs in the direction
          people do not expect — action first, affection following. Christ does not
          tell Ephesus to work up an emotion. He tells them to go and do the things
          they used to do when they loved him, and the ordering implies that the doing
          is a road back to the loving.</p>
        </div>

        <p>This is worth stating plainly for anyone in a dry season: <em>you are not
        required to generate a feeling.</em> You are required to remember, to turn, and
        to resume the practices. The warmth is God's business and it has a way of
        returning to people who are back at the altar putting wood on.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 3: SEVEN CHURCHES
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="seven">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Seven Churches as a Diagnostic</h2>

        <p>The seven letters of Revelation 2–3 are the New Testament's most complete
        catalogue of the ways a believing community goes wrong — and the two exceptions
        are as instructive as the five diagnoses.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Church</th><th>Condition</th><th>The prescription</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Ephesus</strong><br><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 2:1-7">2:1–7</a></td>
              <td>Orthodox, hard-working, discerning — and cold</td>
              <td>Remember, repent, do the first works</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Smyrna</strong><br><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 2:8-11">2:8–11</a></td>
              <td><em>No rebuke.</em> Poor, slandered, about to be imprisoned</td>
              <td>"Do not fear... be faithful unto death"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Pergamum</strong><br><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 2:12-17">2:12–17</a></td>
              <td>Held the faith under pressure, but tolerated corrupting teaching</td>
              <td>Repent — the compromise is not a private matter</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Thyatira</strong><br><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 2:18-29">2:18–29</a></td>
              <td>Growing in love, faith, service — and tolerating a teacher who was leading people into sin</td>
              <td>"Hold fast what you have until I come"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Sardis</strong><br><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:1-6">3:1–6</a></td>
              <td>"You have the reputation of being alive, but you are dead"</td>
              <td>"Wake up, and strengthen what remains" — note that something remains</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Philadelphia</strong><br><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:7-13">3:7–13</a></td>
              <td><em>No rebuke.</em> "You have but little power, and yet you have kept my word"</td>
              <td>"Hold fast what you have, so that no one may seize your crown"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Laodicea</strong><br><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:14-22">3:14–22</a></td>
              <td>Lukewarm; rich and self-sufficient; "you do not realise that you are wretched, pitiable, poor, blind, and naked"</td>
              <td>Buy from me; be zealous and repent — "I stand at the door and knock"</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Two Churches Christ Does Not Rebuke</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Smyrna was poor and persecuted. Philadelphia
          had "little power." Neither had strength, size, influence, or comfort — and
          neither receives a single word of correction.</p>
          <p>Meanwhile the church that thought it needed nothing is told it is naked,
          and the church with the best reputation is told it is dead. If a believer is
          measuring their spiritual state by capacity, energy, or visible fruitfulness,
          Revelation 2–3 dismantles the instrument. <strong>The two commended churches
          would have scored worst on every metric the other five could have used.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <p>One more detail deserves attention. The most famous invitation in the New
        Testament — <em>"Behold, I stand at the door and knock. If anyone hears my
        voice and opens the door, I will come in to him and eat with him"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:20">Revelation 3:20</a>) — is addressed
        not to unbelievers but to the worst church on the list. Christ is outside the
        door of a congregation that bears his name, knocking to be let back in, and
        offering dinner. It is the single warmest sentence spoken to the coldest people
        in the book.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 4: THE ALTAR FIRE
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="altar">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Fire That Was Never Allowed to Go Out</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          The fire on the altar shall be kept burning on it; it shall not go out. The
          priest shall burn wood on it every morning.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Leviticus 6:12">Leviticus 6:12</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The fire on the tabernacle altar was originally kindled by God himself
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Leviticus 9:24">Leviticus 9:24</a>). No priest lit
        it. And yet the standing instruction, repeated three times in a few verses, was
        that it must not go out — and the way it was kept was that somebody carried
        wood to it every single morning, for centuries.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Whole Doctrine in One Picture</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">God lit it. God did not maintain it without
          means. The priest's daily armful of wood did not create the fire and was
          never optional.</p>
          <p>This is Philippians 2:12–13 in Levitical form, and it is the answer to the
          question that runs through this entire study. If you did not light it, you
          cannot take credit for it; if you stop carrying wood, do not be surprised by
          the temperature. The two facts have never been in competition except in
          arguments.</p>
        </div>

        <p>The New Testament uses the same image for the individual believer, twice,
        in the imperative:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For this reason I remind you to fan into flame the gift of God, which is in
          you through the laying on of my hands.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 1:6">2 Timothy 1:6</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The verb <em>anazōpyrein</em> means to rekindle, to stir embers back to
        flame. Paul says it to Timothy — a young pastor, personally discipled by Paul,
        with a genuine gift and a godly family line
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 1:5">1:5</a>). Even he needed the reminder,
        and the letter suggests the reason was fear rather than sin (1:7). And the
        counterpart command names what puts fires out:
        <em>"Do not quench the Spirit"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Thessalonians 5:19">1 Thessalonians 5:19</a>).</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 5: ABIDE
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="abide">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Abiding — the Verb That Is Not a Verb of Effort</h2>

        <p>Part II read John 15 as a warning text. It is also, and primarily, an
        instruction — and the instruction is gentler than the warning's reputation.</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">menō</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">μένω (John 15, eleven times)</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">To remain, stay, dwell, lodge, continue in
          a place. It is the word for staying put — the opposite of leaving, not the
          opposite of resting. A branch does not strain to abide in a vine; it abides
          by not being severed. The command "abide in me" is therefore a command to
          <em>stay</em>, and its natural obedience looks less like effort and more like
          not going anywhere.</span>
        </div>

        <p>The promise attached is the most freeing sentence in the passage and the
        most often skipped: <em>"apart from me you can do nothing"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 15:5">John 15:5</a>). This is usually read as a
        rebuke. In context it is a relief — Jesus has just said that the branch's job
        is not to manufacture fruit but to stay connected to the thing that produces
        it. Fruit is what happens to a branch that stays. It is not an assignment
        given to the branch.</p>

        <p>Which reframes the whole question of keeping the fire. The instruction is not
        <em>burn harder</em>. It is <em>stay where the fuel is</em>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 6: MEANS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="means">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Ordinary Means</h2>

        <p>Scripture is remarkably unspectacular about how people are kept. The
        practices below are the wood carried to the altar every morning. None of them
        is dramatic; all of them are commanded or modelled; and the loss of faith
        almost always begins with the quiet discontinuation of two or three of
        them.</p>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">What God Uses</span>
          <p><strong>Scripture, taken regularly rather than intensely.</strong> "Man
          shall not live by bread alone" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 4:4">Matt
          4:4</a>) sets the frequency by the analogy: daily, ordinary, unremarkable,
          and noticed mainly by its absence. Note that the drifted person's first
          instinct is to wait until they feel like reading. That instinct has the
          causation backwards.</p>
          <p><strong>Prayer, including bad prayer.</strong> "Pray without ceasing"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Thessalonians 5:17">1 Thess 5:17</a>). The
          Psalter authorises complaint, confusion, anger, and exhaustion as prayer
          material. A believer who will only pray when composed will eventually not
          pray.</p>
          <p><strong>The Lord's Table.</strong> "Do this in remembrance of me"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:24-26">1 Cor 11:24–26</a>) — a
          promise delivered to the body, repeatedly, that does not depend on the
          recipient's mood. Part III noted that this is where the Lutheran tradition
          sends the doubting, and the instinct is sound whatever one's sacramental
          theology.</p>
          <p><strong>Confession, out loud, to God and to another person.</strong>
          "Confess your sins to one another and pray for one another, that you may be
          healed" (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 5:16">James 5:16</a>). Sin kept
          secret is sin given a room to grow in;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 32:3-5">Psalm 32:3–5</a> is the clinical
          description — bones wasting, strength dried up, until "I acknowledged my sin
          to you."</p>
          <p><strong>The gathering.</strong> "Not neglecting to meet together, as is
          the habit of some" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:25">Heb 10:25</a>).
          The New Testament has no category for a solitary Christian, and the drift is
          almost always visible here first.</p>
          <p><strong>Service, specifically to people who cannot repay.</strong>
          Hebrews 6:10 names "the love that you have shown for his name in serving the
          saints" as the very evidence that grounded the author's confidence in his
          readers. Doing this reliably produces assurance as a by-product, which is
          not the reason to do it and is worth knowing.</p>
          <p><strong>Rest.</strong> A great deal of what presents as spiritual coldness
          is exhaustion. Elijah under the broom tree
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Kings 19:5-8">1 Kgs 19:5–8</a>) wanted to die,
          and God's first two interventions were a meal and a sleep, twice, before any
          conversation. The order in that passage is a rebuke to a great deal of
          pastoral practice.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">A Practical Note on Restarting</span>
          People who have let these go typically try to resume all of them at once,
          fail within a fortnight, and conclude that something is wrong with them.
          Resume one. Keep it for a month. The Ephesian prescription is "do the works
          you did at first" — not "do everything you have ever been told to do." A
          single practice held for a season does more than a comprehensive regime held
          for nine days.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 7: TOGETHER
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="together">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Why You Cannot Do This Alone</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Take care, brothers, lest there be in any of you an evil, unbelieving heart,
          leading you to fall away from the living God. But exhort one another every
          day, as long as it is called "today," that none of you may be hardened by the
          deceitfulness of sin.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 3:12-13">Hebrews 3:12–13</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>This is the New Testament's most direct statement of how apostasy is
        prevented, and the prescription is not a private discipline. It is
        <strong>other people, talking to you, daily.</strong></p>

        <p>The reason is given in the same sentence: sin is <em>deceitful</em>. Its
        characteristic effect is on the perception of the person doing it. Which means
        that the faculty you would use to detect your own hardening is the faculty
        being compromised — and no amount of private self-examination fixes an
        instrument that is drifting with the thing it is measuring. Hebrews' answer is
        external calibration: someone else, close enough to notice, speaking often
        enough to matter.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Uncomfortable Application</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">If nobody currently has permission to tell you
          that you have gone cold, then you have removed the mechanism Scripture
          prescribes for keeping you warm.</p>
          <p>This is worth acting on today rather than agreeing with in principle. It
          means one named person, with standing to ask real questions, who sees you
          often enough to notice a change. Not a small group in general. A person. The
          drifted state is characterised precisely by the absence of anyone in that
          role, which is why it goes unnoticed for years.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 8: DISCIPLINE
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="discipline">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">When It Hurts: Discipline as Evidence</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          It is for discipline that you have to endure. God is treating you as sons.
          For what son is there whom his father does not discipline? ...For the moment
          all discipline seems painful rather than pleasant, but later it yields the
          peaceful fruit of righteousness to those who have been trained by it.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 12:7-11">Hebrews 12:7–11</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Hebrews makes an argument here that is directly relevant to assurance and is
        rarely used for it: <strong>God's corrective attention is evidence of
        sonship, not evidence against it.</strong> The passage goes so far as to say
        that a person left entirely alone would have grounds for concern (12:8).</p>

        <p>Two cautions, because this teaching is easily abused. It does not license
        reading every hardship as a punishment for a specific sin — the book of Job
        exists largely to demolish that inference, and Jesus rejects it explicitly in
        <a class="ref" data-ref="John 9:3">John 9:3</a> and
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 13:1-5">Luke 13:1–5</a>. And it must never be
        used by anyone else to explain your suffering to you; Hebrews addresses the
        sufferer directly, and the moment it becomes a third party's diagnosis it has
        changed into something Job's friends would recognise.</p>

        <p>What it does offer is a reframe available to the person in it: the painful
        exposure of a sin, the collapse of something you were relying on, the season
        where God seems to be making a point — these are consistent with being a son,
        and Hebrews says they are more consistent with it than with the alternative.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 9: THE WAY BACK
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="back">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Way Back</h2>

        <p>Scripture contains more restoration narratives than warning passages, and
        they are told in unusual detail — as though the point were to leave no one able
        to claim their case is unprecedented.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Prodigal</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The father was watching the road</div>
          <p><em>"But while he was still a long way off, his father saw him and felt
          compassion, and ran and embraced him and kissed him"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 15:20">Luke 15:20</a>). Three details are load-bearing.
          The son's prepared speech is <strong>interrupted</strong> — he never gets to
          the part about being made a hired servant. The father <strong>ran</strong>,
          which for a dignified elder in that culture meant public undignified haste.
          And the son came home for entirely mixed motives — he was hungry — and the
          text does not treat the impurity of his motive as disqualifying. <em>Come
          with the motive you have.</em></p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">David</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The anatomy of a restoration</div>
          <p>Adultery, deception, and arranged killing, followed by roughly a year of
          silence. <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 32:3-4">Psalm 32:3–4</a> describes
          that year from the inside: bones wasting away, strength dried up. Then
          confession, and <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 51">Psalm 51</a> — which asks
          not for a return to his old standing but for a clean heart, a renewed spirit,
          and the restoration of <em>joy</em> (51:12). Note what David does not lose:
          "cast me not away from your presence, and take not your Holy Spirit from me"
          is a prayer, and it is answered. Note also what he does lose: the child dies,
          and the sword never departs from his house. Restoration is real and
          consequences are real, exactly as in Genesis 3.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Peter</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Restored in the same currency as the failure</div>
          <p>Three denials by a fire in a courtyard; three questions by a fire on a
          beach (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 21:15-17">John 21:15–17</a>). Jesus
          does not minimise it — the third question grieves Peter, which is the point,
          because the wound had to be opened to be closed. And every answer is met not
          with a probation but with a <strong>commission</strong>: feed my lambs, tend
          my sheep, feed my sheep. The man who failed most publicly is given the work
          back, in front of witnesses, before he has proved anything.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Jonah</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Sent again, with the same commission, unimproved</div>
          <p>"The word of the LORD came to Jonah <em>a second time</em>"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jonah 3:1">Jonah 3:1</a>) — the same word, to the
          same man, for the same city. And chapter 4 shows he was still bitter
          afterwards. God's willingness to re-send is not contingent on the prophet
          having become admirable.</p>
        </div>

        <p>The Old Testament's standing invitations are as unguarded as anything in the
        New:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Return, faithless Israel, declares the LORD. I will not look on you in anger,
          for I am merciful, declares the LORD; I will not be angry forever.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 3:12">Jeremiah 3:12</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          I will restore to you the years that the swarming locust has eaten.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Joel 2:25">Joel 2:25</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>That last promise is worth pausing on for anyone who believes they have
        wasted too much time to bother returning. It is not "I will forgive the years."
        It is <em>restore</em> — the assumption being that the lost years are precisely
        what the returning person grieves, and God's answer addresses the grief rather
        than dismissing it.</p>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">What Coming Back Actually Involves</span>
          <p><strong>1. Say it, specifically, to God.</strong> "If we confess our sins,
          he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all
          unrighteousness" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 1:9">1 John 1:9</a>). Note
          the grounds offered: <em>faithful and just</em>, not merely kind. Forgiveness
          is presented as something God owes to his own character and to the finished
          work of Christ, which is why it does not depend on how convincingly you
          repent.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Tell one person.</strong> James 5:16. Secrecy is the condition
          under which drift became distance in the first place.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Go back to the room.</strong> Physically return to the
          gathering, before you feel ready, and expect it to be uncomfortable for a
          while.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Resume one practice.</strong> See the note above; not all of
          them.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Make restitution where you can.</strong> Zacchaeus
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 19:8">Luke 19:8</a>) — not to purchase
          anything, but because repentance that touches nothing external is not yet
          finished.</p>
          <p><strong>6. Expect consequences to remain, and do not read them as
          rejection.</strong> David was forgiven and the sword stayed in his house.
          Both are true and neither cancels the other.</p>
          <p><strong>7. Do not wait to feel forgiven before acting as though you
          are.</strong> The feeling is downstream of the practice far more often than
          the reverse.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 10: WAYWARD
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="wayward">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">A Word to the Wayward</h2>

        <p>This section is addressed directly to one kind of reader, and if you are not
        that reader you should not take it. Part IV's table is the place to check.</p>

        <p>If you have read four parts of this study looking for the paragraph that
        would let you off — if what you actually wanted was the sentence proving that
        the life you are living is compatible with the faith you claim — then this is
        the honest answer: <strong>you will not find it here, and you will not find it
        in any of the seven traditions in Part III.</strong> Not one of them. The
        Calvinist, the Wesleyan, the Catholic, the Orthodox, the Lutheran, and even the
        Free Grace teacher all say the same thing to a person living in settled,
        defended, unrepented sin, and none of them says "you are fine."</p>

        <p>And Scripture is more direct than any of them. <em>"Why do you call me
        'Lord, Lord,' and not do what I tell you?"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 6:46">Luke 6:46</a>). <em>"Do not be deceived:
        God is not mocked, for whatever one sows, that will he also reap"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 6:7">Galatians 6:7</a>). <em>"They profess
        to know God, but they deny him by their works"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:16">Titus 1:16</a>).</p>

        <p>Notice what the danger actually is, because it is not what most people
        assume. It is not that God's patience will run out — Scripture presents his
        patience as nearly limitless. It is that <em>yours</em> will change: that the
        capacity to be moved will quietly go. Hebrews calls it being "hardened by the
        deceitfulness of sin" (3:13), and the frightening thing about hardening is that
        it does not hurt. The man who cannot feel conviction any more does not
        experience that as a loss. He experiences it as peace.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Word Is Today</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text"><em>"Today, if you hear his voice, do not
          harden your hearts as in the rebellion"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 3:15">Hebrews 3:15</a>, quoting
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 95:7-8">Psalm 95:7–8</a>).</p>
          <p>The word Hebrews stresses is <strong>today</strong> — used four times in
          the passage. Not because tomorrow is guaranteed to be too late, but because
          the ability to hear is the thing at risk, and it is not distributed evenly
          across time. <strong>If you can still feel the pull of this, that is the
          voice, and the fact that you can hear it is the mercy.</strong> The reason to
          come back today is not that God is about to give up on you. It is that you
          can still hear him today, and that is not a permanent condition.</p>
        </div>

        <p>And then, immediately, the other half, because this study does not end on a
        threat. The door you are being told to come through is not a narrow one and
        there is no queue. The father in Luke 15 was watching the road. Christ in
        Revelation 3 is knocking on the door of the worst church in Asia and offering
        to come in for dinner. The invitation everywhere in Scripture is issued to
        people who have no case to make and are told to come anyway:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Come, everyone who thirsts, come to the waters; and he who has no money, come,
          buy and eat! ...Let the wicked forsake his way, and the unrighteous man his
          thoughts; let him return to the LORD, that he may have compassion on him, and
          to our God, for he will abundantly pardon.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 55:1-7">Isaiah 55:1–7</a></cite>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 11: LAST WORD
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="last">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Last Word Is Not Yours</h2>

        <p>This study has tried to be even-handed, and even-handedness has a limit: at
        some point a believer has to be told where to stand. So here is where, stated
        as plainly as five parts of argument permit.</p>

        <p>You were not saved because you were strong, and you are not kept because you
        are strong. Part I found the pattern in Genesis and it has not changed since:
        God moves first, the covering is provided rather than achieved, the rescue
        precedes the law, and the loyalty required is a loyalty God himself undertakes
        to produce. Part II found that every keeping text in the New Testament grounds
        security outside the believer — in the Father's giving, the Son's intercession,
        the Spirit's sealing — and that not one of them grounds it in the strength of
        your grip. Part III found seven traditions disagreeing about the mechanism and
        agreeing about the counsel. Part IV found that the tests are given so that you
        may <em>know</em>, and that fear of being lost is nowhere listed as a mark of
        the lost.</p>

        <p>What is asked of you is what has always been asked: to hold on to the God
        who has hold of you, and to keep carrying wood to the altar in the morning.
        Both halves. Neither one alone.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Now to him who is able to keep you from stumbling and to present you blameless
          before the presence of his glory with great joy, to the only God, our Saviour,
          through Jesus Christ our Lord, be glory, majesty, dominion, and authority,
          before all time and now and forever. Amen.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 24-25">Jude 24–25</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Jude writes that at the end of a letter about people who fell away. He has
        spent twenty-three verses describing apostasy in the harshest language in the
        New Testament — waterless clouds, wandering stars, twice dead — and then he
        turns to the people still standing and tells them who is holding them. The
        warning and the doxology are the same letter, addressed to the same room, by
        the same author, in that order.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The End of the Matter</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">He is able to keep you from stumbling. You are
          not able to keep yourself. And he has never once dropped anyone who was
          holding on to him — including the ones who were holding on badly, with the
          wrong motives, on the way back from a long way off.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Letters to the Seven Churches of Asia in Their Local Setting</cite>,
          Colin Hemer. JSOT, 1986. <em>The standard work on what the seven letters
          meant to their original cities — Laodicea's water supply, Sardis' acropolis,
          Philadelphia's earthquakes. It changes how the images read.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Celebration of Discipline</cite>, Richard Foster (1978), and
          <cite>Spiritual Disciplines for the Christian Life</cite>, Donald Whitney
          (1991). <em>Two different traditions on the ordinary means; Whitney is the
          more directly biblical, Foster the more imaginative.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Return of the Prodigal Son</cite>, Henri Nouwen. Doubleday, 1992.
          <em>A long meditation on Luke 15 through Rembrandt's painting. The chapters
          on the elder brother are the ones most readers need and least expect.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>A Call to Backsliders</cite> (Sermon 86), John Wesley. <em>Short,
          available free, and almost uniquely direct on the pastoral care of people who
          have gone cold and believe they are beyond return.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

